memory, sched, arch/s390x/cpu, sys, lib, build: memory overhaul, reverse-map/reclaim/OOM stack, CPU source .zcomponent migration, full libstdc++ integration, IPC and policy subsystems

    This change set is the largest restructuring since the initial
    monolithic commit (d42c372).  It reworks the memory subsystem
    end to end — introducing a reverse-map, a bounded page cache,
    unified reclaim with OOM fallback and an explicit object
    ontology backed by SCOMS — retires the hand-rolled C++ support
    library in favour of a vendored, freestanding-capable libstdc++
    consumed through `import std;`, migrates the s390x CPU sources to
    the directory-scoped `.zcomponent` build model, fixes a large
    number of memory and scheduling defects, and lands new IPC and
    policy subsystems.

    * Memory overhaul.  The VM layer was rewritten around an interval
      rb-tree keyed by va_start with explicit split/merge bookkeeping,
      committed-page accounting and a per-domain budget with atomic
      reservation and charge refund.  Mapping teardown no longer
      guesses whether a mapped frame is PMM-owned; it drops PTEs first
      and lets the reverse map release the pin beneath, and the
      vm_txn rollback path now tears the mapping down before returning
      any frames so live PTEs can never alias buddy-owned memory.

    * New reverse map (rmap) stripes a bounded chunked table over the
      physical frame index, giving every page a set of resident mapping
      views, used both for physical-range eviction and for migrate/
      compaction.  A new bounded, hashed, file-backed page cache with
      explicit residency and mapping pins sits above the pager.

    * Unified reclaim walks the page cache and every VMA through the
      object graph, evicts anonymous, resident, non-pinned pages, and
      falls back to a deterministic global OOM selection and kill
      callback when nothing else can be reclaimed.

    * The memory object hierarchy now forms a single SCOMS-backed
      closed enriched union (memory_objects), discriminated by kind,
      that carries VM-region, kmem-cache, page-cache, grant and
      address-space views and powers the global rmap/page-cache table.

    * PMM NUMA grading was corrected: a drawer id is a topology
      coordinate, not a node id, so the boot fallback now resolves to
      the boot node; frames retired after validation or MCCK failure
      are recorded in a pending dead-pool and excluded from the buddy
      until an offline-recovery path can verify them.

    * dom/flat cpu source management migrates to component-scoped
      layout: each cpu facility (ar, aste, clock, cpumask, cputimer,
      diag, features, hotplug, ipi, irq, lowcore, pcp, psw, sclp,
      sigp, smp, stsi, sync, uaccess, ...) now lives in its own
      `<name>.zcomponent/` directory carrying a `manifest.zxd`, a
      `.cxxm` interface module and a `.cxx` implementation, in place
      of the previous single flat `.cxxm` per facility.  This is the
      zx-discovery component model applied to `arch/s390x/cpu`.

    * The hand-rolled `lib/*` support library (array, algorithm,
      concepts, expected, optional, pair, span, string_view, tuple,
      type_traits, variant, compare, util, ...) is removed in favour of
      the vendored ZXFoundation-LIBSTDCXX submodule.  Kernel sources
      now `import std;` and use `std::expected`, `std::optional`,
      `std::move` and the standard algorithmics directly throughout,
      with `-nostdlink++`/`-nostdinc++` retained and the kernel CRTSE
      keeping only the freestanding runtime glue (terminate, pure
      virtual, guard, unwinding hooks).  The C++ extensions option is
      enabled. LLVM-specific flags are added to suppress warnings
      generated by the addition of libstdc++

    * New subsystems: `zxfoundation::sys::ipc` (bounded endpoint and
      message objects with capability and grant attachment transfer),
      delivered through `sys_op::ipc_*` gate operations, and
      `zxfoundation::policy` (nucleus-owned policy-server
      registration).  SCOMS gains the nucleus-owned object metadata
      core (ordered, generation-counted `kobject_table`) and the
      `scoms_ref` shared handle.

    * A large number of memory and scheduler defects were closed.  The
      retired page accounting no longer lets frames re-enter the
      buddy; earlier NUMA node fallback misused the drawer id as a
      node id; the `vm_region` slot was double-constructed and
      wiped its SCOMS id, breaking every legitimately issued region
      capability (BUG-008 class); domain names no longer alias
      transient user buffers and secondary strands are detached before
      their domain's final release (BUG-006/BUG-007 class).  Scheduler
      admission is now a DGP-gated path (`sched.admit`) and
      sched_softirq context work was split into a leaf module.

    * Architecture API: Introduced IBM z/VM-compatible STHYI API and table walker.
      Introduced PTF (PERFORM TOPOLOGY FUNCTION) support. Refactored ECAG, AR, ASTE,
      API are now more reliable and convenient to use.